jon sabo <sabo@criminal.org> wrote:
> 
> I have an lx164 alpha system that uses milo.

It is hard to come up with good reasons to use milo on lx164
(are you dual booting this machine with NT?).  There is an SRM
console for LX and there are technical reasons why it is preferable.
Also the whole installation would be a snap (assuming that an old,
FAT-style, partition table was entirely removed before you are
starting).

> I am using milo 2.2 built 
> against kernel 2.2.19 and I have installed redhat 7.2.  I may have made 
> some mistakes like making some of the file systems ext3 but maybe not. 

These are not mistakes although some planning and a bit of understanding
does help.

> I can not boot now that I have installed redhat 7.2.

If you would bother to check older postings in this group, say via
Google, you would quickly discover that this topic was discussed here
many times even with explanations how to boot.  The short answer is that
unless you have a milo which is capable enough then a default kernel
from 7.2 cannot be booted that way.  Also SCSI drivers, which give you
an access to your disk, are loaded as modules from initrd. OTOH fairly
recent updates to 7.2,
ftp://ftp2.compaq.com/pub/linux/RedHat/7.2-alpha/updates/
include among other things kernel-milo-2.4.18-27.7.x.hp.alpha.rpm and
also an installation "HPWrapper".  Presumably one of these things, or
maybe both, would make dealing with milo vagaries easier.  I did not
have an opportunity to try.

You can update your current installation by booting from an installation
media and switching to a text console before installing anything.
Then you can start your network, or use some other means, and copy
what you need to your system that way.

   Michal


