Following up my own stuff here...

Andreas Feldner wrote:

>> Older Milo's can only deal with kernels which have a start address of
>> 0310000. The most recent Milo I could find can deal with 0810000, but
>> not with 1010000. Anyway, there is an option when compilong the kernel
>> CONFIG_ALPHA_LEGACY_START_ADDRESS which forces it to use the 0310000
>> address. It works great for me.
> 
> That one causes a crash for me directly after "jumping into the kernel".
> My Milo seems to jump into the right entry point, but doesn't appear to
> pass any boot parameters.
> 
> I'm wondering if one could patch the LEGACY_START_ADDRESS to 0810000?

Amazingly stupid an aproach, still it *does* work! 

I patched arch/alpha/vmlinux.lds.in:

#ifdef CONFIG_ALPHA_LEGACY_START_ADDRESS
  . = 0xfffffc0000810000; /* instead of 0xfffffc0000810000 */
#else

and include/asm-alpha/system.h:

#ifdef CONFIG_ALPHA_LEGACY_START_ADDRESS
/*#define KERNEL_START_PHYS     0x0300000 */
                             /* Old bootloaders hardcoded this.  */
#define KERNEL_START_PHYS       0x0800000 
             /* well... newer ones hardcoded THIS! pelzi@flying-snail.de */
#else
#define KERNEL_START_PHYS       0x1000000 
                         /* required: Wildfire/Titan/Marvel */
#endif

And now it actually works! It is beyond me how the non-legacy-compiled
kernel could even start with the wrong entry address, but still it did but
didn't get any boot arguments.

Perhaps this helps someone else, too?

Cheers,
Andreas.



