J�rgen,

You didn't go wrong... I found part of the issue... Here's what has to
happen
for SRM console systems to work properly.

HERE'S THE KICKER... You may have to format the disk to kill off
all previous paritition and partitioning. I found doing a format/verify of
the system disk allows the installer to not have any problems.

THEN, You must setup the disk as BSD partition. You MAY have to do it
manually. If you do, create the first partition (called "a" in BSD) starting
at
cylinder #3 (yes, you want cylinder #1 and #2 blank for a reason I will show
below).  An example is shown here:

[root@localhost src]# fdisk /dev/sda
Detected an OSF/1 disklabel on /dev/sda, entering disklabel mode.
To return to DOS partition table mode, use the 'r' command.

BSD disklabel command (m for help): p

6 partitions:
#       start       end      size     fstype   [fsize bsize   cpg]
  a:        3         6         4       ext2
  b:        7       137       131       ext2
  c:      138       170        33       swap
  d:      171       236        66       ext2
  e:      237       498       262       ext2
  f:      499       521        23       ext2

BSD disklabel command (m for help): q

Where my partitions are:

[root@localhost src]# df
Filesystem           1k-blocks      Used Available Use% Mounted on
/dev/sda2              1035692    133688    849392  14% /
/dev/sda1                31109     12687     16816  44% /boot
/dev/sda6               178892      4129    165526   3% /home
/dev/sda5              2071416   1473748    492444  75% /usr
/dev/sda4               521780    245916    249360  50% /var

Once you partition the disk, the install will at the end of the job run
this:

$ swriteboot    /dev/sda    /boot/bootlx

This writes the aboot boot strap to the very begining (remember
those 2 cylinders we left open!) of the disk. I found that you have
to use this method for RedHat 7.2 on all my systems [I'm an ISP,
I have dozens of Alphas running Linux for various customers].

I also recommend you look at:
http://linux.iol.unh.edu/linux/alpha/docs/redhat-srm.html

It's old, but sure helped me understand how SRM works (I've been
a MILO guy for years).

