> www.fs.tum.de/~bunk/debian potato main)
> But only got tons of "###" at the screen after booting.

I had this a couple of days ago. Check the kernel start address using

readelf -h <uncompressed_kernel> | grep Entry

Look at the last 7 positions of the entry point address. You should
see 0310000, 0810000 or 1010000.

Older Milo's can only deal with kernels which have a start address of
0310000. The most recent Milo I could find can deal with 0810000, but
not with 1010000. Anyway, there is an option when compilong the kernel
CONFIG_ALPHA_LEGACY_START_ADDRESS which forces it to use the 0310000
address. It works great for me.

> So i switched back
> to 2.2.18. Do you know whether make-kpkg builds a zipped kernel (like
> make bzImage)?

I am afraid I do not know, I build my kernels from the kernel source.
bzIamge is something specific for x86, I believe. On Alpha you would
do 'make boot'. This produces the uncompressed vmlinux kernel image in
the top level kernel directory. The compressed image can be found in
arch/alpha/boot/vmlinux.gz.

> I remember, there was also an strange error message, that the kernel
> isn't zipped. And ls -al offered, that the kernel was really not small. 
> So probably not zipped?!

You can find out using

file <kernel_file>

If it says 'gzip compressed data', it's compressed. If it says 'ELF 64
bit LSB executable', it's not.
 
