Debian Alpha runs very nicely on the digital server 5305. My setup is:
Two processors, one 500MHz and one 433MHz, 2GB RAM
Seven SCSI on the same Symbios SCSI card
Millenium II Graphics Card
Debian "Testing" with KDM and KDE 3.1.2 from "unstable"

First of all, if your machine boots into AlphaBios you'll need to change it
to SRM. There's a setting under the advanced options (from memory I think
it involves pushing F2 or F6 from the AlphaBios Menu, it should say along
the bottom of the screen). Save settings and reboot. SRM boots into a blue
screen. Typing "sh dev" at the SRM prompt will tell you the names of all
your drives. Pop Debian Woody CD1 into the drive and type "boot DKA500" if
your cdrom drive shows up as DKA500 (If its a SCSI drive it'll probably be
DKA0, DKA400 or DKA500). The machine will restart and boot the debian
installer.

When fdisk runs to partition the drives, make sure that you use the bsd
disklabel option. I think the installer puts fdisk in this mode by default
but if it doesn't, press 'b' to do it manually. There needs to be a c:
partition which occupies the entire disk but contains no filesystem use a:
b: d: etc.. as you like.

I generally decline to install anything with the woody package manager and
dselect because as soon as I can bootstrap the system under debian i edit
sources.list and run "apt-get dist-upgrade" which gets its packages from
testing rather than the woody cd. But that's a matter of preference.

Anyway, there's enough there to get you started I should think. Let me know
if you run into any snags.

/rob


